We first note that $SO(2n, 2n; \mathbb{Z})$ is generated by the matrices of the following form:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
    P & 0 \\ 0 & P^{-T}
\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}
    \textbf{\textit{I}} & \beta \\ 0 & \textbf{\textit{I}}
\end{pmatrix},
\begin{pmatrix}
    0 & \textbf{\textit{I}} \\ \textbf{\textit{I}} & 0
\end{pmatrix},\quad $$
where $P \in GL(2n, \mathbb{Z})$ and $\beta$ is an antisymmetric integral $2n\times 2n$-matrix. This follows for example from \cite[Theorem 9.2.8]{hahn2013classical}:
These matrices generate all elementary matrices, thus generating the spinoral kernel, and 
\[P = \operatorname{diag}
(-1, 1, \dots, 1)\] 
provides an element with non-trivial spinor norm.

Thus the theorem  follows in three steps.
The first set of generators of $SO(2n,2n;\mathbb{Z})$ correspond to base change by $P$, which induces a natural isomorphism of generalized Dolbeault algebras.
The second set of generators correspond to integral $\beta$-transforms, which do not change the generalized Dolbeault algebra by Lemma \ref{lemmma:integralBeta}.
The last generator corresponds to T-duality relative to a point, which leaves the generalized derived category invariant by Theorem \ref{theorem:inducedequivalences}.

