\label{lemma:transport}
    There is a canonical isomorphism
    \[ \Psi \colon V/(L \cap \tilde L \oplus IL \cap I \tilde L) \xrightarrow{\cong} Z = ((IL \cap I\tilde L)^+)^\perp / (IL \cap I \tilde L)^+,\quad v \mapsto (\pi_{IL}(v),\pi_{I \tilde L}(v)). \]
    Hence, $Z$ naturally carries a complex structure induced by $I$. Moreover, the symplectic form $\omega'$ on $Z$ is of type $(1,1)$ for $I$.
    \begin{proof}
        The map $V \ni v \mapsto (\pi_{IL}(v), \pi_{I\tilde L}(v)) \in IL \oplus I\tilde L$ factors through the subspace $((IL \cap I\tilde L)^+)^\perp$ because $\omega_K|_{IL \times L} = \omega_K|_{I\tilde L \times \tilde L} = 0$. Its kernel is $L \cap \tilde L$, and the preimage of $(IL \cap I\tilde L)^+$ is $IL \cap I \tilde L$. This shows that $\Psi$ is well-defined and injective. For dimensional reasons it is an isomorphism. The form $\Psi^* \omega'$ is given by
        \[ (\Psi^* \omega')(v,v') = \omega_K((\pi_{I \tilde L}-\pi_{IL})(v),v'). \]
        Thus,
        \begin{align*}
            (\Psi^* \omega')(Iv,Iv') &= \omega_K((\pi_{I \tilde L}-\pi_{IL})(Iv),Iv')\\
            &= \omega_K(I(\pi_{\tilde L}-\pi_{L})(v),Iv') \\
            &= -\omega_K(I(\pi_{I\tilde L}-\pi_{IL})(v),Iv') \\
            &= \omega_K((\pi_{I\tilde L}-\pi_{IL})(v),v') =  (\Psi^* \omega')(v,v')
        \end{align*}
        as $\pi_{I\tilde L}-\pi_{IL} = \pi_{L} - \pi_{\tilde L}$ and since $\omega_K$ is of type $(2,0)+(0,2)$ with respect to $I$.
    \end{proof}

